I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule says: "The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text, because a model places its final answer last and marks it: a reply that quotes its previous answer and then gives a corrected one is scored on the correction."

The first reply text is: "```json\n{\"a\":1}\n{\"b\":2}\n```\n{\"c\":3}". The second reply text is: "{\"c\":3}". For the first text, the rule requires the last object inside the code fence, which is {"b":2}. For the second text, the rule requires the last top-level object, which is {"c":3}. The function returns {"c":3} for both texts, because the fenced pool fails to parse the two-object fence as a single JSON object, so it falls back to the top-level scan and picks the last top-level object, which is {"c":3} in both. I would be wrong if recover_json_object returned {"b":2} for the first text and {"c":3} for the second, or if the rule were read to say that a fence containing two objects does not count as a fence that holds one, so the first text should also score the last top-level object.